// The position map: parameters and locals of the function being canonicalised,
// in the order they were first met. A name is answered with its position, so
// renaming every variable in a function cannot change its fingerprint, while
// using one name where another function used two still can.
//
// Only names of the function's own variables ever reach here. Called
// functions, imported globals and exported names are kept verbatim by their
// callers, which is what lets two otherwise identical functions differ by
// which name they mention.

cn(string name) {
  int idx = find_str((import cnames), name);
  if(idx < 0) {
    idx = cn_add(name);
  }
  string ret_s = "" + idx;
} return string ret_s;
given canon_begun_fixture ("first"):("0")
given cn_seeded_fixture ("second"):("1")

cn_add(string name) {
  push((import cnames), name);
  int ret_i = len((import cnames)) - 1;
} return int ret_i;
given canon_begun_fixture ("first"):(0)
given cn_seeded_fixture ("second"):(1)

// A name read as a value. A function's name is a function value and keeps its
// name, as a callee does: two functions that differ only in which function
// they pass are different logic, and giving the name a position would make
// them the same. The recursion marker applies to it for the same reason it
// applies to a call.
canon_var(int id) {
  string name = s1_of(id);
  string out = "fv(" + canon_callee(name) + ")";
  if(is_fn_value(id) == 0) {
    out = "v" + cn(name);
  }
} return string out;
given lz_b ((import lz_var)):("v0")
given cn_seeded_fixture ((import lz_var)):("v1")
